From 3fc1305b421af4da19e58c21d464c91a1ca09330 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: c6 <c6@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 03:55:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] c6: 'go' runs on OS threads, 'co' spawns coroutines

'go' used to be a keyword for "run this async task as a top-level
coroutine".  It now starts a real thread, which is what people expect
from the word, and the coroutine spawn verb is 'co':

    co f(a);        -- f is 'async': run it up to its first real wait
    task t = f(a);  -- same, but keep the handle
    go f(a);        -- f is a plain function: run it on a new thread

'go', 'co' and 'task' are soft keywords: lexing is untouched, so
'int go = 1;', 'go = 2;', 'x = go;', 'go(x);', 'typedef int co;',
'co v;' and 'go:' keep working.  They are recognized where a statement
starts (the next token is already an identifier) unless a typedef of
that name is in scope.  TOK_C6_GO/TOK_C6_TASK are gone from tcctok.h
and the pp unintern loop stops at TOK_C6_AWAIT.

The thread machinery is deliberately small: the arguments are evaluated
once and copied into a heap block laid out with the same alignment loop
in both the spawn site and the trampoline; the trampoline is a
per-translation-unit 'static' function declared lazily at the first
'go' that needs it and defined at end of TU, so prototypes in headers
cost nothing.  Each thread joins the threads it started -- the compiler
emits __c6_th_join_all() at the end of 'main' and tcc_run_tests() calls
it too, so '-run' never frees JIT memory under a running thread.
---
